I premium averaged <b>11.17%</b>, versus 10.29% in August and 18.30% in July. It ranged from 5.52% to 19.91%. The direct MSPI-B/COMEX street premium averaged <b>23.45%</b>, almost unchanged from August's 22.99% and far below July's 45.52%.</p><table><tr><th>Month</th><th>Avg JPMI premium</th><th>Avg street premium</th></tr><tr><td>June</td><td>12.42%</td><td>41.35%</td></tr><tr><td>July</td><td>18.30%</td><td>45.52%</td></tr><tr><td>August</td><td>10.29%</td><td>22.99%</td></tr><tr><td>September</td><td>11.17%</td><td>23.45%</td></tr></table>
<h2>4. Market Layers</h2><p>September exposed a useful distinction between <b>wholesale normalization</b> and <b>retail persistence</b>. Refinery and dealer bids moved down faster than MSPI-B. By month-end RS stood at ¥329.60/g and DB at ¥321.00/g, while MSPI-B remained at ¥379.00/g. This was not a return to July-style dislocation, but it confirms that the Japanese street layer continued to clear at a structurally higher price than wholesale and COMEX-linked benchmarks.</p>
<h2>5. Mercari Retail Market</h2><p>MSPI-B averaged <b>¥401.33/g</b>, with a September high of ¥450.00 and low of ¥349.00. The month finished at ¥379.00/g. Unlike August, however, the price story was accompanied by a meaningful improvement in observed market depth.</p>
<h2>6. Liquidity Analysis</h2><div class="chart"><h3>Mercari Liquidity — Final Daily Snapshot Count</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 900 270" role="img"><line x1="58" y1="34.0" x2="882" y2="34.0" stroke="#263447"/><text x="4" y="38.0" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">3.2</text><line x1="58" y1="82.5" x2="882" y2="82.5" stroke="#263447"/><text x="4" y="86.5" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">2.6</text><line x1="58" y1="131.0" x2="882" y2="131.0" stroke="#263447"/><text x="4" y="135.0" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">2.0</text><line x1="58" y1="179.5" x2="882" y2="179.5" stroke="#263447"/><text x="4" y="183.5" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">1.4</text><line x1="58" y1="228.0" x2="882" y2="228.0" stroke="#263447"/><text x="4" y="232.0" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">0.8</text><polyline points="58.0,131.0 97.2,131.0 136.5,131.0 175.7,131.0 215.0,131.0 254.2,131.0 293.4,131.0 332.7,131.0 371.9,131.0 411.1,131.0 450.4,131.0 489.6,131.0 528.9,131.0 568.1,131.0 607.3,211.8 646.6,131.0 685.8,131.0 725.0,131.0 764.3,50.2 803.5,131.0 842.8,131.0 882.0,131.0" fill="none" stroke="#4da6ff" stroke-width="2.5"/><text x="58" y="18" fill="#4da6ff" font-size="12">● Qualifying sales</text><text x="58.0" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">Sep 01</text><text x="254.2" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">Sep 08</text><text x="450.4" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">Sep 15</text><text x="646.6" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">Sep 22</text><text x="882.0" y="258" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8fa3b8" font-size="11">Sep 30</text></svg></div><div class="grid"><div class="card"><span>Observed sale-days</span><b>22</b></div><div class="card"><span>Daily snapshot total</span><b>44</b></div><div class="card"><span>Average / observed day</span><b>2.00</b></div><div class="card"><span>One-sale days</span><b>1</b></div></div><p>September recorded <b>44 qualifying sales across 22 observed daily snapshots</b>, averaging 2.00 per observed sale-day. Only one day finished with a one-sale snapshot. That compares with August's 37 sales across 20 observed days (1.85/day, seven one-sale days) and July's 38 across 22 days (1.73/day, six one-sale days). The improvement matters: September's lower prices were accompanied by <i>better</i> transaction depth, unlike the late-July pattern where falling street prices coincided with thinning activity.</p><p class="delta">Liquidity counts use the final MSPI snapshot for each observed UTC date. They are a daily-snapshot activity measure, not a fully deduplicated transaction ledger.</p>
<h2>7. JPMI Market Assessment</h2><div class="callout"><div class="quote">“September normalized the market's depth more than its premium.”</div><p>JPMI's composite premium remained above August on average, while the retail premium was essentially stable. Yet the Mercari layer became less fragile: more qualifying observations were present and one-sale days fell sharply. This combination argues against describing September as simple premium compression. A better characterization is <b>moderate persistent premium with improving retail liquidity</b>.</p></div>
<h2>8. October Outlook</h2><p>The base case entering October is a more balanced physical market than the one observed in July and August. The key test is whether the improved Mercari transaction count persists if COMEX volatility returns. If retail liquidity remains near two or more qualifying sales per observed day while the street premium stays above 20%, that would strengthen the case that the premium is structural rather than merely a symptom of thin trading. Conversely, a renewed fall in transaction count would make individual MSPI-B observations less representative of broad clearing conditions.</p><p>Three conditions deserve particular attention: continued RS/DB alignment with COMEX; whether MSPI-B holds a premium despite stronger transaction depth; and whether a renewed paper-market move produces immediate or delayed transmission into Japanese street prices.</p>
