<h2>10. Methodology & Notes</h2><p>JPMI-AG is a composite measure of observable Japanese physical silver pricing layers. RS represents refinery buyback pricing, DB represents dealer/pawn bid pricing, and MSPI-B represents qualifying sold 1 oz bullion coin observations from Mercari. COMEX is converted to JPY/g using contemporaneous USD/JPY data. Premium figures compare JPMI-AG or MSPI-B with the corresponding COMEX JPY/g benchmark.</p><p>MSPI-B is an observed street-market indicator rather than a formal exchange fixing. Thin daily samples can produce volatility; for that reason JPMI evaluates price together with transaction activity. Raw observations should be retained alongside any adjusted/outlier-filtered series used in future research.</p>
<h2>11. Conclusion</h2><p>September closed with JPMI-AG at <b>¥343.20/g</b> and a JPMI premium of <b>10.75%</b>. Prices weakened across the Japanese physical complex, but the retail layer fell less than refinery and dealer bids. More importantly, Mercari liquidity improved materially: the daily snapshot average returned to 2.00 qualifying sales and one-sale days almost disappeared.</p><p><b>The September message is therefore not simply that premiums persisted. It is that they persisted while observed retail liquidity improved.</b> That makes the premium signal more credible than it was during the thin late-July and August periods and gives October a cleaner benchmark for testing whether Japan's street-market premium is becoming structural.</p>
